.TH AUTOLINT 1 "2026-08-10" "checkrun" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
autolint \- lint supported files through Checkrun policy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B autolint
.RB [ --fix ]
.RB [ --json ]
.RI [ -h | --help ]
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B autolint
.RB [ --fix ]
.RB [ --json ]
.BI --files0-from " manifest|-"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B autolint
lints each supplied file with the Checkrun-selected fast linter for that
filetype. Ignored files, missing files, and files whose linter is not
installed are skipped rather than treated as hard failures.
Files without a backend linter still receive
.B typos
spelling checks, when available, and any configured schema validation.
.PP
When
.B --json
is used, diagnostics are emitted as structured records for editor adapters.
Human-readable output is reserved for terminal use.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --fix
Allow supported linters to apply safe fixes.
.TP
.B --json
Print JSON diagnostic records instead of human-readable output.
.TP
.BI --files0-from " file|-"
Read paths from a NUL-delimited regular file, or from standard input when the
operand is
.BR - ,
instead of positional arguments. Every record must be non-empty and
NUL-terminated. This option cannot be combined with positional files.
.TP
.B --
End option parsing and treat every later argument as a file path.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show usage and supported file types.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHECKRUN_AUTOLINT_JOBS
Maximum number of lint jobs to run concurrently.
.TP
.B CHECKRUN_AUTOLINT_REPORT
File that receives
.B findings=0
or
.B findings=1
when the run completes. Because exit status 2 outranks ordinary findings, a
caller that may tolerate tool failures reads this to learn whether findings
were hidden:
.B findings=1
means some lint step or file reported a status other than 0 or 2. The file is
truncated when the run starts and never completed when a signal ends the run,
so only a completed run can say
.BR findings=0 .
If a finding cannot be recorded, the run exits 125.
.TP
.B CHECKRUN_CONFIG_DIR
Directory for local Checkrun ignore and schema policy. Defaults to
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/checkrun
when the XDG root is absolute, or
.I ~/.config/checkrun
otherwise.
.TP
.B CHECKRUN_PYTHON
Python interpreter used by Python-backed Checkrun helpers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Linting completed, or all files were skipped for non-fatal reasons.
.TP
.B 1
One or more selected linters reported diagnostics or failed without a distinct
tool-failure status, or required lint infrastructure such as
.B yq
or
.B jq
was unavailable.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, registry planning failure, or a selected linter that could not
run, such as
.B typos
rejecting its config. This status outranks ordinary lint findings; see
.B CHECKRUN_AUTOLINT_REPORT
to learn whether any were hidden.
.TP
.B 125
Autolint could not collect or record a result: an internal manifest or scratch
file could not be written, a lint worker's status was lost, a finding could
not be recorded in a
.B CHECKRUN_AUTOLINT_REPORT
that was writable when the run started, the plan named an adapter this version
does not provide, or the temporary plan directory could not be removed. It
outranks findings and status 2.
.TP
.B 127
A required runtime dependency, such as a suitable Python interpreter, is missing.
.TP
.BR 129 ", " 130 ", " 143
The run was cancelled by SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM, respectively (128 plus the
signal number). Lint workers that autolint started in the background are
stopped before it exits.
.PP
Ordinary findings usually exit 1, but a linter's own nonzero status other
than 2 is passed through, so treat any status not listed here as findings.
A listed status can also come from a linter: one that itself exits 125 is
treated as an internal failure, and one killed by a signal can report 129,
130 or 143.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR checkrun (1),
.BR autoformat (1)
